#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Reference delivery driver of the Fixture City Oracle (T19, af-iefuud).

It makes one ticket of a marked convoy walk the delivery flow in a throwaway
city, one event per call, so that bin/fixture-city-oracle can observe the
public state after each. Called only as:

    delivery-driver --workspace DIR prepare [--inject NAME] [--project NAME] [--answer NAME]
    delivery-driver --workspace DIR publish
    delivery-driver --workspace DIR advance
    delivery-driver --workspace DIR stop

`prepare` builds the city (the Factory settings, the product of the Delivery
Fixture City in a Git repository with its Validated Prototype, an empty
tracker) and prints where its public state is read: the convoy, the ticket,
the repository and the environment of the state commands. `publish` leaves
what the publication door leaves: the ticket and its convoy in the tracker,
the convoy marked (bin/convoy-state mark), the integration branch of its
Spec opened (bin/spec-integration open). Each `advance` plays exactly one
event and prints `{"event": ..., "idle": ...}`: either one pass of the
Factory's order (the real bin/convoy-advance, which starts, has reviewed and
evaluated, measures the fidelity, merges and closes, then runs the end of
the Spec), or what one agent session leaves behind on the ticket at work:
the walked one, then any `repair` the order published in the convoy. `idle`
is true when the event changed nothing. `stop` stops whatever product an
evaluation left launched, and closes an Acceptance Session left open (T28).

No agent runs here. The driver writes what an author session leaves (the
criterion tests and their table, the commits, the Ticket Handoff it says),
in the worktree of the ticket; each state of the author's step is written by
the real gate that is responsible for it (T37, af-fg8lhx): bin/ticket-author
start and demonstrate, bin/tdd-step red, freeze and green, bin/test-review
review (answered by the stand-in of its suite, tests/fixtures/test-review/
reviewer, through the real bin/test-review record) and bin/ticket-exit exit.
The driver never writes one of these states through bin/convoy-state: the
oracle refuses a walk where it does. The commands are the ones the oracle
hands over in FIXTURE_CITY_FLOW_BIN, which it records. The code
reviewer and the Evaluator are the stand-ins of their own suites
(tests/fixtures/code-review/reviewer, tests/fixtures/ticket-evaluation/
evaluator), which answer through the real bin/code-review and
bin/ticket-evaluation; so are the Evaluator and the reviewer of the end
(tests/fixtures/outcome-evaluation/evaluator, tests/fixtures/spec-end-review/
reviewer). The tracker is the fake one of tests/fixtures/convoy-state/gc.
The product is launched on a loopback port by the real bin/project-launch,
so this driver is not run inside the no-socket sandbox of the planning
adapters.

`--inject` makes the walk go wrong in one named way:
    criterion-edited   a criterion is edited on the ticket once its author has finished
    closed-by-hand     the ticket is closed in the tracker, outside the ticket-end gate
    three-failures     the convoy holds two more tickets (T20, af-y9pw9z): one whose author
                       session ends three times without a Ticket Exit, and one that waits
                       for it; the walked ticket waits for neither
    story-not-observed the Spec holds a second user story (the list of the items in JSON at
                       GET /items) that the walked ticket covers and the product does not
                       serve (T26, af-vwcflt). The graph is published by the real publication
                       door (bin/ticket-graph-publish bd-publish, on the fake `bd` of
                       tests/fixtures/ticket-graph-publish, which shares the tracker file),
                       so that the order can publish the `repair` the Evaluator of the end
                       drafts through the patch door; the author session of that repair
                       makes the product serve GET /items. `publish` then names the convoy
                       and the ticket, which the door numbered. First project only
    proposition        the same Spec and the same door as `story-not-observed`, but the
                       Evaluator of the end observes both user stories on the home page, so
                       the Outcome is Delivered without a round; the author of the walked
                       ticket leaves one finding « hors ticket » in its Ticket Handoff, which
                       the Evaluator of the end classifies as a « proposition »; and the
                       project the ticket targets declares its human approver (T29,
                       af-mcd7pw). The author session of a `repair` the order publishes
                       afterwards makes the product serve GET /items. First project only

`--project` names the project of the fixture the ticket belongs to (T35,
af-f62cxt); the same events are played on either, each from its own profile:
    delivery-fixture-city       the first one (default): a web page in Python, with a Validated Prototype;
                                its ticket is an interface ticket
    delivery-fixture-city-api   the second one: a JSON API on Node.js, no web page, no prototype

`--answer` makes the walk go on past the Delivered Outcome (T30, af-vlzc6v):
the project declares its human approver, and the event that follows the
pass that recorded the Outcome Delivered is his answer, played by the driver
as the operator through the real bin/owner-answer (`owner:accepted`,
`owner:abandoned`). The pass after it follows the answer by itself
(bin/outcome-promote):
    none        nobody answers (default): the walk settles on the Delivered Outcome
    accepted    a verdict « sans suite » on each « proposition » left without one, then
                « accepté » with his words, on the commit and the dossier of the Delivered Outcome
    abandoned   « abandonné », with its one-line reason
"""
